---
import AIImage from "@jonasmpi/astro-ai-disclosure/AIImage.astro";
import manifest from "virtual:ai-image-manifest";

import abstractAIVisual from "../../assets/abstract-ai-visual.webp";
import ScenarioLayout from "../../layouts/ScenarioLayout.astro";

/**
 * Not one of these usages passes an `ai` prop. The declaration comes from
 * `src/assets/abstract-ai-visual.webp.ai.json`, matched to the image by its own
 * absolute path.
 */
const entries = Object.entries(manifest);
---

<ScenarioLayout
  title="Sidecar metadata"
  intro="Metadata can live in a .ai.json file next to the asset instead of on every usage. The integration scans them at build time and exposes them as virtual:ai-image-manifest."
  expectation="The first image carries a badge although the page passes no ai prop — it comes from the sidecar. The second passes an inline ai prop, which wins over the sidecar."
>
  <h2>Sidecar only — no <code>ai</code> prop on the page</h2>
  <p class="note">
    The sidecar declares <code>scope: "creative-work"</code>, which is in scope under
    <code>eu-article-50</code> as well as the site's <code>all-ai</code>.
  </p>
  <AIImage
    src={abstractAIVisual}
    alt="Abstract illustration of an industrial AI system"
    widths={[640]}
    sizes="640px"
    format="webp"
  />

  <h2>Inline metadata wins over the sidecar</h2>
  <p class="note">
    Same image, but the page states something more specific for this usage. The badge reads
    <em>Mit KI-Unterstützung</em>, not the sidecar's <em>Von KI erzeugt</em>.
  </p>
  <AIImage
    src={abstractAIVisual}
    alt="Abstract illustration of an industrial AI system"
    widths={[640]}
    sizes="640px"
    format="webp"
    ai={{ kind: "assisted", scope: "creative-work", description: "Inline override for this usage" }}
  />

  <h2>What the manifest contains</h2>
  <p class="note">
    Keys are absolute source paths, because that is what Astro reports as an image's
    <code>fsPath</code> in dev and in a production build alike — unlike <code>src</code>, which is
    hashed in a build. Matching on the path means two same-named images in different folders keep
    their own declarations.
  </p>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Image</th><th>kind</th><th>scope</th><th>provider</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      {
        entries.map(([path, disclosure]) => (
          <tr>
            <td>
              <code>{path.split("/src/").pop()}</code>
            </td>
            <td>
              <code>{disclosure.kind}</code>
            </td>
            <td>
              <code>{disclosure.scope}</code>
            </td>
            <td>{disclosure.provider ?? "—"}</td>
          </tr>
        ))
      }
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>Malformed sidecars fail the build</h2>
  <p class="note">
    A sidecar with an unknown <code>kind</code>, a misspelled field or invalid JSON stops the build
    and names the file. A declaration the author got wrong is worse than none, so it is never
    silently ignored.
  </p>
</ScenarioLayout>

<style>
  h2 {
    margin-top: 2.25rem;
    font-size: 1.05rem;
  }

  .note {
    max-width: 46rem;
    color: var(--muted);
    font-size: 0.9rem;
  }

  table {
    width: 100%;
    max-width: 46rem;
    border-collapse: collapse;
    font-size: 0.88rem;
  }

  th,
  td {
    padding: 0.4rem 0.6rem;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border);
    text-align: left;
  }

  th {
    color: var(--muted);
    font-size: 0.75rem;
    text-transform: uppercase;
  }
</style>
